This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation:

\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
&\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
\quad x\in\mathbb{R}^4,
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $x=(x_1,x_2)$ with $x_1\in\mathbb{R}$ and $x_2\in\mathbb{R}^3$, $\beta,\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$, and the nonlinearity satisfies certain specified conditions. Using the same method as in Bao et al. 2021, we prove the existence of a normalized ground state solution under the specified conditions. Note that the maximum value of $\mu$ can be $6$ in this context. The research is influenced by the works of Li et al. 2018, Chen et al. 2019, and Bao et al. 2019, and 2021. The study is also related to existing findings by Szulkin and Shao 2017. The research provides key insights into the characteristics of the normalized ground state solution. The findings are also notable as the first to address the issue for the biharmonic Choquard equation under the specified critical nonlinearity conditions. The research has significant implications for the study of critical points and its further examination. The study offers important suggestions for future research in the field. The critical value of $\mu$ can be $6$ and it plays a crucial role in the research. The study has significant research potential and it is related to various existing findings. The research has significant implications for the study of critical points and its further examination. The study offers essential insights into the characteristics of the normalized ground state solution. The research has significant research potential and it is related to various existing findings. The study provides key characteristics of the normalized ground state solution. The study is notable for addressing the issue for the biharmonic Choquard equation under the specified critical nonlinearity conditions. The research has significant implications for the study of critical points and its further examination. The study offers important suggestions for future research in the field. The study is also related to existing findings by Szulkin and Shao 2017. The research provides key insights
